arospkg 0.3.1 -- a package manager for AROS One x86_64
=======================================================

Two programs, one package database:

  apkg         the command-line client, used from the Shell
  PkgManager   a Zune window over the same library: browse, install, upgrade,
               roll back, remove

Both install programs from AROS Archives, check every download against a
small public index, and remove them again without deleting files you have
changed.

New in 0.3.1:

  * TLS through Mbed TLS 3.6.7 (TLS 1.2 and 1.3), compiled in, instead of
    the SDK's OpenSSL 1.1.0h; the programs are about a quarter of the size.
    The randomness for TLS comes from jitterentropy-library 3.7.0 on AROS
    One; if it is unusable on a machine, apkg refuses to connect and never
    falls back to anything weaker.
  * A download whose connection breaks says so ("the connection to HOST
    broke after N of M bytes: connection reset") instead of "the download
    is the wrong size". Nothing partial ever reaches the cache.
  * In a terminal that says what it can display (AROS XTerm and Limpet),
    `show`, `search` and `install` use bold, coloured verdicts, the
    window's width and a progress line that updates in place. In an
    ordinary Shell window apkg prints as before, with a progress line every
    10% of a download. Output redirected to a file and --json are
    unchanged.

0.3 brought `apkg show` and the ARexx port in PkgManager; 0.2 upgrade and
rollback, verify, dry runs and the window.


WHICH ARCHIVE
-------------

Each archive is for one CPU and ABI; a program built for one does not start
on another.

  arospkg-0.3.1.x86_64-aros-v11.zip   AROS One and other ABIv11 systems:
                                      apkg and PkgManager. This README is
                                      written for it.
  arospkg-0.3.1.x86_64-aros-v1.zip    mainline AROS (ABIv1): apkg only
  arospkg-0.3.1.i386-aros-v0.zip      the i386 ABIv0 line: apkg only
  arospkg-0.3.1.aarch64-aros.zip      hosted aarch64 (Macaros): apkg only

Only the AROS One archive is fully tested. The others are experimental; the
release notes on GitHub say what was checked for each. The catalogue offers
x86_64 ABIv11 packages only, so on the other targets search hides them and
install refuses them.


WHAT YOU NEED
-------------

  * AROS One 1.3, x86_64. This binary is built for ABIv11, the ABI used by
    AROS One. It does NOT run on mainline AROS (ABIv1): a program built for
    one ABI does not start on the other.
  * crt.library and stdlib.library. Both ship with AROS One. PkgManager
    also needs muimaster.library (Zune), which AROS One ships.
  * A working TCP/IP stack (AROSTCP) for `apkg update` and for downloads.
    Searching, listing, removing and recovery work offline.
  * The certificate bundle ENV:SYS/Certificates/ca-bundle.crt. AROS One ships
    it. Without it apkg refuses to download anything -- an unverified download
    is exactly what it exists to prevent.
  * Room on SYS: for the packages you install, in SYS:Packages.


INSTALLING apkg
--------------

  1. Unpack this archive anywhere. AROS One ships UnZip:

         UnZip arospkg-0.3.1.x86_64-aros-v11.zip
  2. Copy apkg to C: so the Shell can find it:

         Copy arospkg-0.3.1.x86_64-aros-v11/apkg C:

     PkgManager can stay where you unpacked it, or go to SYS:Utilities;
     start it from Wanderer or from the Shell. It looks for apkg's records
     in SYS:Packages, so both programs see the same installed packages.

  3. Check it starts and reports the right ABI:

         apkg --version

     The line "abi v11" must be there.
  4. Fetch the index:

         apkg update

apkg creates SYS:Packages itself on first use. Nothing else on your system is
changed by installing apkg.


USING IT
--------

  apkg search [word]    what the index offers; matches name, summary, category
  apkg install <id>     download, verify, install
  apkg list             what is installed
  apkg show <id>        what a package is, whether it is compatible with this
                        machine, what it needs, what is installed. Changes
                        nothing and downloads nothing.
  apkg info <id>        the record of one installed package
  apkg remove <id>      uninstall, keeping anything you changed
  apkg upgrade <id>     to the index's newer revision of the package; prints
                        the plan first and refuses if a file you changed
                        would have to go
  apkg rollback <id>    back to the revision the last upgrade replaced
  apkg verify <id>      compare the installed files with apkg's record: as
                        installed, changed locally, missing, not the
                        package's. It repairs nothing.
  apkg requires <id>    what the package needs from the system, probed now
  apkg doctor           say what is unresolved after an interruption, and why
  apkg doctor --retry   finish or undo an interrupted operation
  apkg --help           everything, including the testing options

  --dry-run             with install, remove, upgrade or rollback: print the
                        plan and change nothing. The plan may be incomplete
                        when the archive is not in the cache yet; --fetch
                        allows the download and nothing else.
  --json                search, list and verify print rows for programs
  --plain               no colour, bold or progress line, even in a
                        terminal that can show them
  --color=auto|always|never
                        colour only. Output to a file is never decorated

For example:

  apkg search game
  apkg install soliton
  apkg --dry-run upgrade soliton
  apkg upgrade soliton
  apkg rollback soliton
  apkg remove soliton

Close a program before you upgrade, roll back or remove it. apkg replaces
the drawer underneath a running program and the program will not notice.

PkgManager shows the same packages as a list with a category cycle, a search
field and a details pane. The buttons at the bottom do what the commands
above do; before an upgrade or a rollback it shows the plan in a window of
its own and asks.
While one operation runs, the window cannot be closed and apkg refuses to
run against the same root, so the two cannot interfere.


COMPATIBILITY
-------------

`apkg show` and the details pane say whether a package is compatible with
this machine: "native" when it is built for this CPU and ABI, "incompatible"
when it is built for another, "undetermined" when the index does not say
enough. That is a judgement from the index, not a test that the program
runs. The requirements it needs from the system are a separate line, probed
on the machine when you ask. apkg refuses to install a package that is not
native here, before downloading it. When the index lists more than one build
of a program for this machine, it refuses to pick one. An upgrade stays on
the build that is installed.

`apkg search` and the window list only packages that are native here, and
say how many they left out.


SCRIPTING PkgManager WITH AREXX
-------------------------------

While PkgManager runs it has an ARexx port, PKGMANAGER. A script can check
whether a program is installed, open its details in the window, install or
remove it and read the real outcome, follow the progress, and ask for an
upgrade or a rollback. Those two still wait for you to confirm them in the
window. Five examples are in Examples/ARexx:

  rx Examples/ARexx/IsInstalled.rexx soliton
  rx Examples/ARexx/Install.rexx soliton

The commands, their answers and their errors are described in
docs/arexx.md in the source repository.

One thing to know: the ARexx interpreter in AROS One puts a command's result
text into RC, so a script cannot tell success from RC there. The examples
show the way that works: RESULT is set after success, and LASTERROR says why
a command failed.


WHERE THINGS GO
---------------

  SYS:Packages/<id>/        the program's drawer
  SYS:Packages/<id>.info    its drawer icon, when the package has one
  SYS:Packages/db/          apkg's own records -- do not edit
  SYS:Packages/cache/       downloaded archives; the previous revision of an
                            upgraded package stays in cache/previous/ so it
                            can be rolled back

Open SYS:Packages in Wanderer and start a program from its drawer. Some
programs look for their data relative to the current directory, so from a
Shell, CD into the drawer first:

  CD SYS:Packages/<id>


ARCHIVE FORMATS
---------------

apkg installs from ZIP and from LHA, the Amiga's own format, and decides which
is which by looking inside the file rather than at its name. An archive that is
neither is refused and says so.

Whatever the format, every member is checked before anything is written: a path
that is absolute (including the AROS way, a ':' anywhere, which names a volume),
one that escapes with '..', or one over the size limit rejects the whole
archive. One upload in AROS Archives really does carry paths like ram:thing/,
and apkg refuses it rather than writing into RAM:.


WHAT HAS BEEN TESTED
--------------------

The index lists 27 packages. Each was installed by apkg, started, and removed
again, on AROS One 1.3 x86_64 running in QEMU. "Started" means the program
opened its window or printed its output and was running; it is not a claim
that every feature of every program works.

apkg itself was tested on the same machine, on a persistent disk: interruption
at every point an operation can be stopped, including inside an upgrade and a
rollback, write failures, recovery that is itself interrupted, a real reboot in
the middle of an install, and downloads that must be refused (wrong
certificate, plain http, no certificate bundle). Upgrade and rollback were run
on a real program (Soliton), which started before, between and after them.
Both programs were also run from a plain Shell with its default 40960-byte
stack: apkg asks the system for a larger one itself, so no `stack` command
is needed before it.
PkgManager was driven through install, upgrade, rollback, a refused upgrade and
removal on the same machine, and its ARexx port through every command,
concurrent operations, cancelling, closing the window during an operation and
running out of memory. The run reports are in the source repository under
docs/reports/.

For 0.3.1 the whole regression was run again on AROS One with the new TLS,
together with downloads whose connection was made to break (reported as a
broken connection, nothing cached, the next attempt succeeding, also in
the same PkgManager window), and the new output was checked in Limpet on a
dark and a light theme, including resizing the window during a download.


LIMITS -- READ THESE
--------------------

  * `apkg upgrade` moves between revisions of the SAME upstream version: a
    repackaging by the index, marked "-aros2" and so on. A new upstream
    version is a different package build; when the index carries one, remove
    the old and install the new. No version strings are compared.
  * Rollback goes back exactly one step, to the revision the last upgrade
    replaced, and only while that archive is still in cache/previous/.
  * Remove and install again is NOT an upgrade. Removal keeps files you have
    changed, in the package's drawer, and lists them in
    SYS:Packages/db/doctor/. While that drawer exists, installing the same
    package again is refused, with a message saying why. Move the drawer away
    (keeping what you need from it), then install again.
  * A drawer icon you changed -- Workbench rewrites one when you snapshot the
    drawer's window -- is kept on removal for the same reason, and reported.
  * No dependencies between packages are resolved. apkg does check that the
    libraries a package needs from the system are present (for example
    SDL.library) and refuses before downloading if they are not.
  * Nothing is installed into LIBS:, FONTS: or other system directories.
    Optional extras some programs describe in their own documentation (extra
    fonts, catalogs) are not installed.
  * The AROS One archive is the tested one; the mainline, i386 and aarch64
    archives are experimental (see WHICH ARCHIVE).
  * No 32-bit (ABIv0) programs. AROS One ships the EmuV0 emulator, but on
    the machine these were tested on it did not start any program, so
    apkg treats such packages as not compatible here.
  * The index is not signed. Its SHA-256 lines prove a download is the file
    the index names, not who wrote the index.
  * The index is metadata only. The programs themselves are downloaded from
    AROS Archives, where their authors published them; apkg does not host or
    copy them. A hash says nothing about the quality or safety of the program.


IF SOMETHING WAS INTERRUPTED
----------------------------

Every install, removal, upgrade and rollback is journalled. If one is interrupted -- a crash, a
reset, power loss -- the next command that changes anything finishes or undoes
it before doing anything else. `apkg doctor` says what it found; `apkg doctor
--retry` runs that recovery on demand. There is no "force": if something on
disk cannot be accounted for, apkg leaves it where it is and says so.


CHECKSUMS
---------

BUILD.txt in this archive names the source commit and the SHA-256 of the
programs in it; `apkg --version` prints the SHA-256 of the running binary,
which must match the line for apkg. The SHA-256 of each archive is in
SHA256SUMS on the release page.


